IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hdsc-dates`

-- Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) -- the questions of the person making a complaint.
--
-- SEAT: the complainant: the user of a health or disability service (the patient, the person treated, the research
-- subject), or the person complaining for them (a representative, a carer, a provider), who brings a complaint to the
-- Director of the Health and Disability Services Complaints Office. "Can I complain about this?", "By when must I
-- complain?", "Will my complaint be rejected?", "What happens to it next, and by when?", "Can what is said in
-- conciliation be used in my court case?", "What if I sue?". The Director, the conciliator and the Minister get no seat:
-- their opinions and decisions are asked as facts.
--
-- Every exported question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a
-- DEONTIC or an EVENT. The duties with deadlines that the complainant meets (s 24, complaining within 24 months; s 27(5),
-- giving more information within the time the Director fixes) and the provider's duty the complainant waits on (s 51,
-- reporting remedial action within 45 days) are regulative rules run inside questions.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 3 (health service, excluded service, user), s 4(4); Part 3 (ss 19-22, 24-30, 32A, 33, 34, 35,
-- 36A(3)); Part 3A (ss 36B, 36C, 36BA, 37, 39, 40(4), 41, 42, 43); s 44(2); Part 3C (ss 50(2), 51, 52A(1)); Part 3F (ss 52,
-- 58); the offences a complainant meets or is protected by (ss 21, 36A(6), 51, 52G, 52N, 52Q, 61, 62, 71, 72, 73), and s 71
-- as a duty of confidence. Out, with reasons, in NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA), Act No. 75 of 1995, compilation including the
-- amendments of the Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Amendment Act 2022 (No. 35 of 2022), retrieved
-- 7 September 2026, deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_46372.txt. No regulations are deposited (prescribed
-- services, codes of conduct, prescribed offences and prescribed circumstances are asked as facts).
--
-- Sourced from the Western Australian Legislation website, State of Western Australia, CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to the Director are INPUTS ("if the Director is
-- satisfied that ..."). Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is
-- the text's own no is marked "(the text's no)"; an answer where the text is limited by its own wording or silent is
-- marked "NOTHING-kind" (or is NOTHING in a MAYBE answer).

§ `Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995: the questions of the person making a complaint`

§§ `s 3 -- Is it a health service?`

-- s 3(1) "health service means any service provided by way of (a) ... and (b) ... and (ba) ... and (bb) ... and (c) ...
-- and (d) ..., and includes any (e) ... (h) prescribed service, but does not include an excluded service". The paragraphs
-- are joined by "and" but read as alternatives (finding 1). "excluded service means a health service that is provided
-- without remuneration in a rescue or emergency situation". Paragraph (g) (a service within (a) to (c) by a person who
-- holds out as providing health care) adds nothing for the person receiving it: the service is already within (a) to (c).
DECLARE `Kind of service` IS ONE OF
    `diagnosis or treatment of a physical or mental disorder or injury, or a suspected one (s 3 health service (a))`
    `health care, including palliative care and voluntary assisted dying (s 3 health service (b))`
    `prescribing or dispensing a drug, a medicinal preparation or an aid for therapeutic use (s 3 health service (ba), (bb))`
    `a preventive health care programme, such as screening or immunisation (s 3 health service (c))`
    `medical or epidemiological research (s 3 health service (d))`
    `an ambulance service (s 3 health service (e))`
    `a surgical or related service (s 3 health service (ea))`
    `a welfare service complementary to a health service (s 3 health service (f))`
    `a service ancillary to another health service that affects the people receiving it (s 3 health service (ga))`
    `a service prescribed by regulations (s 3 health service (h))`
    `none of the kinds listed`

DECLARE `Is it a health service` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is a health service under s 3, so a complaint about it can be made under this Act`
    `no: it was provided without payment in a rescue or emergency, so it is an excluded service and not a health service (s 3, the text's no); the Director may refer a complaint about it elsewhere with your written consent (s 28)`
    `no: it is not a service of any kind listed in the definition of health service (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`

@export Is the service I want to complain about a "health service" under the Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) (s 3)? Diagnosis, treatment, health care, prescribing or dispensing, screening and immunisation, medical research, ambulance, surgical, complementary welfare and ancillary services count, but not a service given free in a rescue or emergency.
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of service` @desc What the service was
      `free in a rescue or emergency` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service was provided without payment (remuneration) in a rescue or emergency situation, such as first aid by a bystander
GIVETH AN `Is it a health service`
`s 3 -- is it a health service` `the kind` `free in a rescue or emergency` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the kind` EQUALS `none of the kinds listed`
        THEN `no: it is not a service of any kind listed in the definition of health service (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`
        IF `free in a rescue or emergency`
         THEN `no: it was provided without payment in a rescue or emergency, so it is an excluded service and not a health service (s 3, the text's no); the Director may refer a complaint about it elsewhere with your written consent (s 28)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: it is a health service under s 3, so a complaint about it can be made under this Act`

§§ `ss 19-22 and 25 -- Who may complain, and about what`

-- s 25(1): "A complaint must allege that one or more of the following has occurred". The list is closed by "must": a
-- complaint alleging none of them does not comply with the Act (s 26(1)(c)), the text's no.
DECLARE `What my complaint is about` IS ONE OF
    `s 25(1)(a): a provider acted unreasonably by not providing a health service for the user`
    `s 25(1)(b): a provider acted unreasonably in the manner of providing a health service for the user`
    `s 25(1)(c): a provider acted unreasonably in providing a health service for the user`
    `s 25(1)(d): a provider acted unreasonably by denying or restricting the user's access to the user's records`
    `s 25(1)(e): a provider acted unreasonably in disclosing or using the user's health records or confidential information`
    `s 25(1)(f): a manager acted unreasonably in investigating or acting on the user's complaint to an institution`
    `s 25(1)(g): a provider charged the user an excessive fee or otherwise acted unreasonably about a fee`
    `s 25(1)(h): a provider that is an applicable organisation failed to comply with the Carers Charter`
    `s 25(1)(i): a health care worker failed to comply with a code of conduct that applies to them`
    `s 25(1)(j): a health care worker failed to comply with an interim prohibition order`
    `s 25(1)(k): a health care worker failed to comply with a prohibition order`
    `none of the matters in s 25(1)`

GIVEN `the matter` IS A `What my complaint is about`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a matter in s 25(1)(i) to (k)` `the matter` IF
        `the matter` EQUALS `s 25(1)(i): a health care worker failed to comply with a code of conduct that applies to them`
    OR  `the matter` EQUALS `s 25(1)(j): a health care worker failed to comply with an interim prohibition order`
    OR  `the matter` EQUALS `s 25(1)(k): a health care worker failed to comply with a prohibition order`

GIVEN `the matter` IS A `What my complaint is about`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a matter in s 25(1)(j) or (k)` `the matter` IF
        `the matter` EQUALS `s 25(1)(j): a health care worker failed to comply with an interim prohibition order`
    OR  `the matter` EQUALS `s 25(1)(k): a health care worker failed to comply with a prohibition order`

GIVEN `the matter` IS A `What my complaint is about`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a matter in s 25(1)(a) to (h)` `the matter` IF
        (NOT (`a matter in s 25(1)(i) to (k)` `the matter`))
    AND (NOT (`the matter` EQUALS `none of the matters in s 25(1)`))

-- s 19 and s 3 "user": who the person complaining is.
DECLARE `Who I am` IS ONE OF
    `the user: I used or received the health service, or was the subject of the research`
    `a person the user chose to complain on the user's behalf`
    `a person the user did not choose, complaining for a user who has died or cannot complain or choose`
    `a carer of the user`
    `a provider complaining for the user about a health service another provider gave the user`
    `a person who only arranged the health service for the user`
    `any other person`

DECLARE `May I make this complaint` IS ONE OF
    `yes: as the user you may complain about this matter (s 19(1)(a))`
    `yes: any person may complain about this matter (s 19(3))`
    `yes: as the user's representative recognised by the Director (s 19(1)(b), s 20)`
    `yes: as a carer you may complain that the Carers Charter was not complied with (s 19(2))`
    `yes: as a provider for whom s 22 applies (s 19(1)(c), s 22)`
    `no: the Director may not recognise you as a representative, because you are paid for it or have a financial interest in the outcome and are not a relative (s 20(3), the text's no)`
    `no: a person the user did not choose can be recognised only if the user has died or cannot complain or choose, and you have a sufficient interest (s 20(2), the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `no: a provider may complain for the user only if the user has died or it would be difficult or impossible for the user to complain (s 22, the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `no: a person who only arranged the service is not a user (s 3 user, the text's no); you may complain as the user's representative if the Director recognises you`
    `no: a complaint must allege one of the matters in s 25(1) (s 25(1), s 26(1)(c), the text's no)`
    `not yet: you may complain as the user's representative once the Director recognises you (s 19(1)(b), s 20(1); the Act lists no other route: NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 19 does not list you for this matter; you may complain as the user's representative if the Director recognises you (NOTHING-kind)`

@export May I make this complaint to the Director of the Health and Disability Services Complaints Office (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) ss 19-22, 25)? The user, a representative the Director recognises, or a provider (where the user has died or cannot complain) may complain about how a provider treated the user; a carer may complain about the Carers Charter; anyone may complain that a health care worker broke a code of conduct or a prohibition order.
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `What my complaint is about` @desc What your complaint says happened (s 25(1))
      `who I am` IS A `Who I am` @desc Who you are in relation to the user (the person who used or received the health service)
      `unpaid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you complain for the user: whether you act without payment (remuneration) for it
      `a relative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you complain for the user: whether you are a relative of the user (including a de facto partner)
      `a financial interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you complain for the user: whether you have a financial interest in the outcome of the complaint
      `user died or unable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the user did not choose you: whether the user has died or, in the Director's opinion, cannot complain and cannot choose someone to complain for them
      `sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the user did not choose you: whether, in the Director's opinion, you have a sufficient interest in the subject of the complaint
      `recognised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you complain for the user: whether the Director has recognised you as the user's representative (or will, if you ask)
      `user died or it is hard to complain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a provider: whether the Director is satisfied that the user has died or that, because of the user's health or situation, it would be difficult or impossible for the user to complain
GIVETH A `May I make this complaint`
`ss 19-22 -- may I make this complaint` `the matter` `who I am` `unpaid` `a relative` `a financial interest` `user died or unable` `sufficient interest` `recognised` `user died or it is hard to complain` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the matter` EQUALS `none of the matters in s 25(1)`
        THEN `no: a complaint must allege one of the matters in s 25(1) (s 25(1), s 26(1)(c), the text's no)`
        IF `a matter in s 25(1)(i) to (k)` `the matter`
         THEN `yes: any person may complain about this matter (s 19(3))`
        IF `who I am` EQUALS `the user: I used or received the health service, or was the subject of the research`
         THEN `yes: as the user you may complain about this matter (s 19(1)(a))`
        IF `who I am` EQUALS `a person the user chose to complain on the user's behalf`
         THEN `as a representative`
        IF `who I am` EQUALS `a person the user did not choose, complaining for a user who has died or cannot complain or choose`
         THEN IF `user died or unable` AND `sufficient interest`
              THEN `as a representative`
              ELSE `no: a person the user did not choose can be recognised only if the user has died or cannot complain or choose, and you have a sufficient interest (s 20(2), the text's no: its "if" fails)`
        IF `who I am` EQUALS `a carer of the user`
         THEN IF `the matter` EQUALS `s 25(1)(h): a provider that is an applicable organisation failed to comply with the Carers Charter`
              THEN `yes: as a carer you may complain that the Carers Charter was not complied with (s 19(2))`
              ELSE `s 19 does not list you for this matter; you may complain as the user's representative if the Director recognises you (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF `who I am` EQUALS `a provider complaining for the user about a health service another provider gave the user`
         THEN IF `user died or it is hard to complain`
              THEN `yes: as a provider for whom s 22 applies (s 19(1)(c), s 22)`
              ELSE `no: a provider may complain for the user only if the user has died or it would be difficult or impossible for the user to complain (s 22, the text's no: its "if" fails)`
        IF `who I am` EQUALS `a person who only arranged the health service for the user`
         THEN `no: a person who only arranged the service is not a user (s 3 user, the text's no); you may complain as the user's representative if the Director recognises you`
        OTHERWISE `s 19 does not list you for this matter; you may complain as the user's representative if the Director recognises you (NOTHING-kind)`
    WHERE
        -- s 20(3): "The Director is not to recognize a person as a representative unless the person satisfies the
        -- Director that he or she (a) is acting without remuneration; and (b) except in the case of a relative of the
        -- user, has no financial interest in the outcome of the complaint."
        `as a representative` MEANS
            IF (NOT `unpaid`) OR (`a financial interest` AND (NOT `a relative`))
            THEN `no: the Director may not recognise you as a representative, because you are paid for it or have a financial interest in the outcome and are not a relative (s 20(3), the text's no)`
            ELSE IF `recognised`
                 THEN `yes: as the user's representative recognised by the Director (s 19(1)(b), s 20)`
                 ELSE `not yet: you may complain as the user's representative once the Director recognises you (s 19(1)(b), s 20(1); the Act lists no other route: NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `s 24 -- Time limit for complaints`

-- s 24: "The Director must reject a complaint about an incident which occurred more than 24 months before the complaint
-- is made unless in the Director's opinion the person who made the complaint has shown good reason for the delay."
-- A complaint made on the day 24 months after the incident is not "more than 24 months" after it: that day is the last
-- day (fork F1). The good reason is the Director's opinion, asked as a fact.

@export What is the last day on which I can complain about an incident without having to show good reason for the delay (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) s 24)? The Director must reject a complaint about an incident more than 24 months old unless you show good reason for the delay.
GIVEN `the incident` IS A DATE @desc The day the incident you are complaining about occurred
GIVETH A DATE
`s 24 -- the last day to complain without showing good reason` `the incident` MEANS `24 months after` `the incident`

DECLARE `Complainant` IS ONE OF `the person complaining`
DECLARE `Complaint step` IS ONE OF
    `make the complaint to the Director`
    `give the Director the further information required`

-- s 24 as a duty with a deadline for the complainant: complain within 24 months of the incident, or be rejected.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Complainant` `Complaint step`
`s 24 -- complain within 24 months of the incident` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the person complaining`
    MUST `make the complaint to the Director`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the person complaining` BECAUSE "complaint made more than 24 months after the incident: the Director must reject it unless good reason for the delay is shown (s 24)"

-- Runs a complaint rule over what happened. On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not
-- BREACH, so FULFILLED means in time and anything else late. Not exported; it takes no DEONTIC from outside.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 24 -- complaining on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 24 -- complain within 24 months of the incident` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the person complaining` `make the complaint to the Director` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Is my complaint in time` IS ONE OF
    `in time: made no more than 24 months after the incident (s 24)`
    `still in time: not yet made, and 24 months since the incident have not passed (s 24)`
    `late, but the Director finds you have shown good reason for the delay, so s 24 does not require rejection`
    `the Director must reject it: more than 24 months after the incident, without good reason for the delay (s 24, the text's no)`

@export Is my complaint in time (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) s 24)? A complaint about an incident that occurred more than 24 months before the complaint is made must be rejected unless, in the Director's opinion, you have shown good reason for the delay.
GIVEN `the incident` IS A DATE @desc The day the incident you are complaining about occurred
      `made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already made the complaint to the Director (orally or in writing)
      `made on` IS A DATE @desc The day you made it (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet complained)
      `good reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in the Director's opinion, you have shown good reason for the delay (only matters if you are late)
GIVETH AN `Is my complaint in time`
`s 24 -- is my complaint in time` `the incident` `made` `made on` `today` `good reason` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `made` AND (`s 24 -- complaining on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day made`)
        THEN `in time: made no more than 24 months after the incident (s 24)`
        IF (NOT `made`) AND (NOT ((`s 24 -- the last day to complain without showing good reason` `the incident`) `is earlier than` `today`))
         THEN `still in time: not yet made, and 24 months since the incident have not passed (s 24)`
        IF `good reason`
         THEN `late, but the Director finds you have shown good reason for the delay, so s 24 does not require rejection`
        OTHERWISE `the Director must reject it: more than 24 months after the incident, without good reason for the delay (s 24, the text's no)`
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the number of days from` `the incident` `until` (`s 24 -- the last day to complain without showing good reason` `the incident`)
        `the raw day` MEANS `the number of days from` `the incident` `until` `made on`
        -- A complaint dated before the incident is counted as made on day 0.
        `the day made` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

GIVEN `the incident` IS A DATE, `made on` IS A DATE, `good reason` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 24 requires rejection` `the incident` `made on` `good reason` IF
    `s 24 -- is my complaint in time` `the incident` TRUE `made on` `made on` `good reason`
        EQUALS `the Director must reject it: more than 24 months after the incident, without good reason for the delay (s 24, the text's no)`

§§ `s 27 -- How to complain, and further information`

-- s 27(5): "The Director may require a person who complains to give more information about the complaint within a
-- time fixed by the Director." s 27(6): "If a person does not comply with a requirement of the Director under
-- subsection (2), (3) or (5), the Director may reject the complaint." Not an offence: the consequence is that the Director
-- may reject the complaint.
GIVEN `the days fixed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Complainant` `Complaint step`
`s 27(5) -- give the further information within the time fixed` `the days fixed` MEANS
    PARTY `the person complaining`
    MUST `give the Director the further information required`
    WITHIN `the days fixed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the person complaining` BECAUSE "further information not given within the time the Director fixed: the Director may reject the complaint (s 27(6))"

GIVEN `the days fixed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 27(5) -- giving it on this day is in time` `the days fixed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 27(5) -- give the further information within the time fixed` `the days fixed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the person complaining` `give the Director the further information required` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Have I given the further information in time` IS ONE OF
    `yes: given within the time the Director fixed (s 27(5))`
    `not yet, and there is still time: give it by the last day the Director fixed (s 27(5))`
    `no: not given within the time the Director fixed, so the Director may reject your complaint (s 27(6))`

@export The Director has asked me for more information about my complaint within a fixed time: have I given it in time, and what if I have not (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) s 27(5), (6))? If you do not give it within the time fixed, the Director may reject the complaint.
GIVEN `asked on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Director required the further information
      `the days fixed` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days the Director gave you, counted from that day
      `given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given the information
      `given on` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave it (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet given it)
GIVETH A `Have I given the further information in time`
`s 27 -- have I given the further information in time` `asked on` `the days fixed` `given` `given on` `today` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `given`
        THEN IF `s 27(5) -- giving it on this day is in time` `the days fixed` `the day given`
             THEN `yes: given within the time the Director fixed (s 27(5))`
             ELSE `no: not given within the time the Director fixed, so the Director may reject your complaint (s 27(6))`
        IF (`asked on` `plus days` `the days fixed`) `is earlier than` `today`
         THEN `no: not given within the time the Director fixed, so the Director may reject your complaint (s 27(6))`
        OTHERWISE `not yet, and there is still time: give it by the last day the Director fixed (s 27(5))`
    WHERE
        `the raw given day` MEANS `the number of days from` `asked on` `until` `given on`
        `the day given` MEANS IF `the raw given day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw given day`

§§ `ss 24, 26, 27, 28 and 30 -- Will my complaint be rejected?`

DECLARE `Will my complaint be rejected` IS ONE OF
    `it is about an excluded service: the Director may refer it to an appropriate person or body with your written consent (s 28)`
    `the Director must reject it: more than 24 months after the incident, without good reason for the delay (s 24)`
    `the Director must reject it: in the Director's opinion it is vexatious, trivial or without substance, or does not warrant further action (s 26(1)(a), (b))`
    `the Director must reject it: in the Director's opinion it does not comply with this Act (s 26(1)(c))`
    `the Director must reject it to the extent it raises an issue already decided by a court, the State Administrative Tribunal, an industrial tribunal or a registration board (s 26(2))`
    `the Director may reject it: you did not confirm it in writing, give your name and identity details, or give the further information required (s 27(6))`
    `the Director may reject it: the Director is not satisfied that reasonable steps were taken to resolve the matter with the provider (s 30)`
    `none of ss 24, 26, 27, 28 and 30 stands in the way: the Director must decide whether to accept it within 28 days of receiving it (s 34(1))`

@export Will the Director have to reject my complaint, or be able to (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) ss 24, 26, 27, 28, 30)? The Director must reject a late complaint without good reason, one that is vexatious, trivial, without substance, not warranting action or not complying with the Act, and an issue a court or tribunal has already decided; and may reject one where you did not give what was asked or did not first try to resolve it with the provider.
GIVEN `excluded service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service was provided without payment in a rescue or emergency (an excluded service)
      `the incident` IS A DATE @desc The day the incident occurred
      `made on` IS A DATE @desc The day you made (or will make) the complaint
      `good reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in the Director's opinion, you have shown good reason for any delay beyond 24 months
      `vexatious or no action warranted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in the Director's opinion, the complaint is vexatious, trivial or without substance, or does not warrant any further action
      `does not comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in the Director's opinion, the complaint does not comply with the Act (for example, you are not someone who may make it, or it alleges none of the matters in s 25(1))
      `already decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an issue in your complaint has already been decided by a court, the State Administrative Tribunal, an industrial tribunal or a registration board (a National Health Practitioner Board)
      `about undecided matters` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether, in the Director's opinion, the complaint relates to matters that body did not decide
      `did what s 27 required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you confirmed an oral complaint in writing, gave your name and the identity details asked for, and gave any further information within the time fixed, when the Director required it
      `reasonable steps` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Director is satisfied that you (or someone for you) took reasonable steps to resolve the matter with the provider first
GIVETH A `Will my complaint be rejected`
`ss 24-30 -- will my complaint be rejected` `excluded service` `the incident` `made on` `good reason` `vexatious or no action warranted` `does not comply` `already decided` `about undecided matters` `did what s 27 required` `reasonable steps` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `excluded service`
        THEN `it is about an excluded service: the Director may refer it to an appropriate person or body with your written consent (s 28)`
        IF `s 24 requires rejection` `the incident` `made on` `good reason`
         THEN `the Director must reject it: more than 24 months after the incident, without good reason for the delay (s 24)`
        IF `vexatious or no action warranted`
         THEN `the Director must reject it: in the Director's opinion it is vexatious, trivial or without substance, or does not warrant further action (s 26(1)(a), (b))`
        IF `does not comply`
         THEN `the Director must reject it: in the Director's opinion it does not comply with this Act (s 26(1)(c))`
        IF `already decided` AND (NOT `about undecided matters`)
         THEN `the Director must reject it to the extent it raises an issue already decided by a court, the State Administrative Tribunal, an industrial tribunal or a registration board (s 26(2))`
        IF NOT `did what s 27 required`
         THEN `the Director may reject it: you did not confirm it in writing, give your name and identity details, or give the further information required (s 27(6))`
        IF NOT `reasonable steps`
         THEN `the Director may reject it: the Director is not satisfied that reasonable steps were taken to resolve the matter with the provider (s 30)`
        OTHERWISE `none of ss 24, 26, 27, 28 and 30 stands in the way: the Director must decide whether to accept it within 28 days of receiving it (s 34(1))`

§§ `s 34 -- What happens to my complaint once it is accepted`

DECLARE `What happens to my accepted complaint` IS ONE OF
    `the Director must investigate it (s 34(5A))`
    `the Director must attempt a negotiated settlement, refer it for conciliation, or investigate it (s 34(5))`
    `the Director must attempt a negotiated settlement or refer it for conciliation (s 34(4)(a), (b))`
    `the Director must attempt a negotiated settlement (s 34(4)(a))`
    `the Director must refer it for conciliation (s 34(4)(b))`
    `the Director must investigate it, as it is suitable for neither settlement nor conciliation and an investigation is warranted (s 34(4)(c))`
    `no further action: the Director must tell you so in writing (s 34(6)(b))`
    `this question assumes a complaint about one of the matters in s 25(1)`

@export My complaint has been accepted: what must the Director do with it (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) s 34(4)-(6))? For a complaint about how a provider treated the user, the Director must try to settle it by negotiation, refer it to conciliation, or investigate it if neither suits and an investigation is warranted; a code of conduct complaint can go any of those ways; a prohibition order complaint must be investigated.
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `What my complaint is about` @desc What your complaint says happened (s 25(1))
      `suitable for settlement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in the Director's opinion, the complaint is suitable for a negotiated settlement (Part 3A Division 1)
      `suitable for conciliation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in the Director's opinion, the complaint is suitable for conciliation (Part 3A Division 2)
      `investigation warranted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in the Director's opinion, an investigation is warranted, taking into account its likely costs and benefits
GIVETH A `What happens to my accepted complaint`
`s 34 -- what happens to my accepted complaint` `the matter` `suitable for settlement` `suitable for conciliation` `investigation warranted` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the matter` EQUALS `none of the matters in s 25(1)`
        THEN `this question assumes a complaint about one of the matters in s 25(1)`
        IF `a matter in s 25(1)(j) or (k)` `the matter`
         THEN `the Director must investigate it (s 34(5A))`
        IF `a matter in s 25(1)(i) to (k)` `the matter`
         THEN `the Director must attempt a negotiated settlement, refer it for conciliation, or investigate it (s 34(5))`
        IF `suitable for settlement` AND `suitable for conciliation`
         THEN `the Director must attempt a negotiated settlement or refer it for conciliation (s 34(4)(a), (b))`
        IF `suitable for settlement`
         THEN `the Director must attempt a negotiated settlement (s 34(4)(a))`
        IF `suitable for conciliation`
         THEN `the Director must refer it for conciliation (s 34(4)(b))`
        IF `investigation warranted`
         THEN `the Director must investigate it, as it is suitable for neither settlement nor conciliation and an investigation is warranted (s 34(4)(c))`
        OTHERWISE `no further action: the Director must tell you so in writing (s 34(6)(b))`

§§ `Deadlines on my complaint (ss 32A-52)`

DECLARE `Step on my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `s 32A: the Director tells me a National Board is dealing with my complaint (28 days from the Board beginning to deal with it)`
    `s 33(a): the Director tells me my complaint has been referred to another body (28 days from the referral)`
    `s 34(1): the Director decides whether to accept my complaint (28 days from receiving it, extendable by up to 28 days)`
    `s 35(1): the Director gives the provider written notice of accepting my complaint (14 days from deciding to accept it)`
    `s 36A(3): the provider gives its written response (28 days from receiving the notice, or longer if the Director extends it)`
    `s 36B(3): negotiation ends, and the Director must refer the complaint for conciliation or investigate it (56 days from complying with s 35, or longer if extended)`
    `s 37(1): the conciliator gives me written notice of the referral for conciliation (14 days from the referral)`
    `s 40(4)(a): I get a copy of the conciliator's final report (the same day it is made to the Director)`
    `s 40(4)(b): the provider's registration board gets a copy of the conciliator's final report (30 days from the report)`
    `s 44(2): the Director gives the provider written notice of an investigation (14 days from commencing it)`
    `s 50(2): the Director gives me written notice of the decision after an investigation (14 days from the decision)`
    `s 51(1): the provider reports what it has done to remedy the matter (45 days from receiving the s 50(2) notice, extendable by up to 15 days)`
    `s 52(1): the Director gives me written notice of stopping dealing with an issue (14 days from stopping)`

@export By what day must this step on my complaint happen (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) ss 32A-52)? Gives the last day for each time limit the Act sets on a complaint, counted from the day the step's period starts, with any extension the Director has allowed (capped where the Act caps it). No answer where the Director has extended the s 34 decision period for a delay in getting information, as the Act sets no limit on that extension.
GIVEN `the step` IS A `Step on my complaint` @desc Which step you want the last day for
      `the period starts` IS A DATE @desc The day the step's period starts (as stated in brackets in the step: for example the day the Director received your complaint, or decided to accept it)
      `extension days` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days by which the Director extended the period (0 if none); only s 34(1) (up to 28), s 36A(3), s 36B(3) and s 51(1) (up to 15) can be extended
      `extended for a delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For s 34(1) only: whether the Director extended the period because of a delay in getting information the Director asked for (s 34(3))
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`ss 32A-52 -- the last day for this step` `the step` `the period starts` `extension days` `extended for a delay` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the step` EQUALS `s 34(1): the Director decides whether to accept my complaint (28 days from receiving it, extendable by up to 28 days)`
        THEN IF `extended for a delay`
             THEN NOTHING
             ELSE JUST (`the period starts` `plus days` (28 PLUS `capped at` 28))
        IF `the step` EQUALS `s 36A(3): the provider gives its written response (28 days from receiving the notice, or longer if the Director extends it)`
         THEN JUST (`the period starts` `plus days` (28 PLUS `the extension`))
        IF `the step` EQUALS `s 36B(3): negotiation ends, and the Director must refer the complaint for conciliation or investigate it (56 days from complying with s 35, or longer if extended)`
         THEN JUST (`the period starts` `plus days` (56 PLUS `the extension`))
        IF `the step` EQUALS `s 51(1): the provider reports what it has done to remedy the matter (45 days from receiving the s 50(2) notice, extendable by up to 15 days)`
         THEN JUST (`the period starts` `plus days` (45 PLUS `capped at` 15))
        IF `the step` EQUALS `s 40(4)(a): I get a copy of the conciliator's final report (the same day it is made to the Director)`
         THEN JUST `the period starts`
        IF `the step` EQUALS `s 40(4)(b): the provider's registration board gets a copy of the conciliator's final report (30 days from the report)`
         THEN JUST (`the period starts` `plus days` 30)
        IF    `the step` EQUALS `s 32A: the Director tells me a National Board is dealing with my complaint (28 days from the Board beginning to deal with it)`
            OR `the step` EQUALS `s 33(a): the Director tells me my complaint has been referred to another body (28 days from the referral)`
         THEN JUST (`the period starts` `plus days` 28)
        OTHERWISE JUST (`the period starts` `plus days` 14)
    WHERE
        `the extension` MEANS IF `extension days` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `extension days`
        `capped at` `the cap` MEANS IF `the extension` GREATER THAN `the cap` THEN `the cap` ELSE `the extension`

@export If this time limit on my complaint was missed, does the Act say that what was done is still valid (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) s 58)? Missing the time limits in ss 33, 34(1), 35(1), 37, 40(4) and 50(2) does not affect the validity of anything done under the Act; for the others the Act does not say.
GIVEN `the step` IS A `Step on my complaint` @desc Which step's time limit was missed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 58 -- is what was done still valid if this time limit was missed` `the step` MEANS
    IF    `the step` EQUALS `s 33(a): the Director tells me my complaint has been referred to another body (28 days from the referral)`
       OR `the step` EQUALS `s 34(1): the Director decides whether to accept my complaint (28 days from receiving it, extendable by up to 28 days)`
       OR `the step` EQUALS `s 35(1): the Director gives the provider written notice of accepting my complaint (14 days from deciding to accept it)`
       OR `the step` EQUALS `s 37(1): the conciliator gives me written notice of the referral for conciliation (14 days from the referral)`
       OR `the step` EQUALS `s 40(4)(a): I get a copy of the conciliator's final report (the same day it is made to the Director)`
       OR `the step` EQUALS `s 40(4)(b): the provider's registration board gets a copy of the conciliator's final report (30 days from the report)`
       OR `the step` EQUALS `s 50(2): the Director gives me written notice of the decision after an investigation (14 days from the decision)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 51 -- The provider's report on remedial action`

-- s 51(1): "Within 45 days after a provider receives notice under section 50(2), or before the end of any extension of
-- time granted by the Director, the provider must report in writing to the Director what action the provider has taken
-- to remedy the matter. Penalty: $2 500." s 51(2), (3): the provider may ask within the 45 days; the Director may extend
-- by no more than 15 days. s 52A(1): if the notice includes action the provider ought to take and the provider does not
-- report, the Director must give the Minister a copy of the notice and a written report.
DECLARE `Provider` IS ONE OF `the provider`
DECLARE `Provider step` IS ONE OF `report in writing to the Director what it has done to remedy the matter`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Provider` `Provider step`
`s 51(1) -- the provider reports on remedial action within 45 days` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the provider`
    MUST `report in writing to the Director what it has done to remedy the matter`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the provider` BECAUSE "no report on remedial action within 45 days (or the extension): an offence, penalty $2 500 (s 51(1))"

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 51 -- reporting on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 51(1) -- the provider reports on remedial action within 45 days` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the provider` `report in writing to the Director what it has done to remedy the matter` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Has the provider reported in time` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the provider reported within the time allowed (s 51(1))`
    `not yet, and the provider still has time to report (s 51(1))`
    `no: the provider did not report in time, an offence with a penalty of up to $2 500 (s 51(1)); the Director must give the Minister the notice and a report on the failure (s 52A(1))`
    `no: the provider did not report in time, an offence with a penalty of up to $2 500 (s 51(1))`

@export After the Director decided my complaint was justified, has the provider reported in time on what it has done to remedy the matter (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) ss 51, 52A)? The provider must report within 45 days of receiving the decision, or within an extension of up to 15 days it asked for within those 45 days.
GIVEN `notice received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the provider received the Director's written notice of the decision (s 50(2))
      `asked in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider asked the Director for more time within the 45 days
      `extension days` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of extra days the Director allowed (0 if none; the Act allows no more than 15)
      `reported` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider has reported to the Director
      `reported on` IS A DATE @desc The day it reported (if it has not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when the provider has not reported)
      `notice says what to do` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Director's notice says what action the provider ought to take to remedy the matter
GIVETH A `Has the provider reported in time`
`s 51 -- has the provider reported in time` `notice received on` `asked in time` `extension days` `reported` `reported on` `today` `notice says what to do` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `reported` AND (`s 51 -- reporting on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the report day`)
        THEN `yes: the provider reported within the time allowed (s 51(1))`
        IF (NOT `reported`) AND (NOT ((`notice received on` `plus days` `the days allowed`) `is earlier than` `today`))
         THEN `not yet, and the provider still has time to report (s 51(1))`
        IF `notice says what to do`
         THEN `no: the provider did not report in time, an offence with a penalty of up to $2 500 (s 51(1)); the Director must give the Minister the notice and a report on the failure (s 52A(1))`
        OTHERWISE `no: the provider did not report in time, an offence with a penalty of up to $2 500 (s 51(1))`
    WHERE
        `the granted days` MEANS
            IF (NOT `asked in time`) OR `extension days` LESS THAN 0
            THEN 0
            ELSE IF `extension days` GREATER THAN 15
                 THEN 15
                 ELSE `extension days`
        `the days allowed` MEANS 45 PLUS `the granted days`
        `the raw report day` MEANS `the number of days from` `notice received on` `until` `reported on`
        `the report day` MEANS IF `the raw report day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw report day`

§§ `s 29 -- Withdrawing my complaint`

DECLARE `If I withdraw my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `the Director must stop dealing with it and, if the provider was given notice, tell the provider (s 29(1))`
    `the Director has decided to go on dealing with it, as it alleges a breach of a code of conduct or a prohibition order; the Director need not give you further information about it (s 29(2), (3))`

@export If I withdraw my complaint, must the Director stop (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) s 29)? You may withdraw at any time by telling the Director, who must then stop, except that a complaint about a health care worker's breach of a code of conduct or a prohibition order may be carried on.
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `What my complaint is about` @desc What your complaint says happened (s 25(1))
      `the Director proceeds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a complaint about a code of conduct or a prohibition order: whether the Director has decided to go on dealing with it despite the withdrawal
GIVETH AN `If I withdraw my complaint`
`s 29 -- if I withdraw my complaint` `the matter` `the Director proceeds` MEANS
    IF `a matter in s 25(1)(i) to (k)` `the matter` AND `the Director proceeds`
    THEN `the Director has decided to go on dealing with it, as it alleges a breach of a code of conduct or a prohibition order; the Director need not give you further information about it (s 29(2), (3))`
    ELSE `the Director must stop dealing with it and, if the provider was given notice, tell the provider (s 29(1))`

§§ `ss 39, 41 and 43 -- Conciliation and settling`

DECLARE `May someone represent me in conciliation` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Director has determined that conciliation will not work effectively without representation (s 39(1))`
    `no: neither you nor the provider may be represented in conciliation unless the Director so determines (s 39(1), the text's no); others may still attend if the conciliator thinks they will help (s 39(2))`

@export May someone represent me in the conciliation of my complaint (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) s 39)? Neither the person who complained nor the provider may be represented unless the Director determines that the process will not work effectively without it.
GIVEN `the Director determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Director has determined that the conciliation will not work effectively without representation
GIVETH A `May someone represent me in conciliation`
`s 39 -- may someone represent me in conciliation` `the Director determined` MEANS
    IF `the Director determined`
    THEN `yes: the Director has determined that conciliation will not work effectively without representation (s 39(1))`
    ELSE `no: neither you nor the provider may be represented in conciliation unless the Director so determines (s 39(1), the text's no); others may still attend if the conciliator thinks they will help (s 39(2))`

DECLARE `We have resolved it ourselves` IS ONE OF
    `you must tell the Director without delay (s 41(2)); once the Director or conciliator knows, they must stop dealing with the complaint (s 41(3))`
    `the Director or conciliator, now aware, must stop dealing with the complaint (s 41(3))`

@export The provider and I have resolved the matter by agreement: what must I do (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) s 41)? You may settle at any time; you must then tell the Director without delay, and the Director or conciliator must stop dealing with the complaint.
GIVEN `told the Director` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already told the Director that the matter is resolved
GIVETH A `We have resolved it ourselves`
`s 41 -- we have resolved it ourselves` `told the Director` MEANS
    IF `told the Director`
    THEN `the Director or conciliator, now aware, must stop dealing with the complaint (s 41(3))`
    ELSE `you must tell the Director without delay (s 41(2)); once the Director or conciliator knows, they must stop dealing with the complaint (s 41(3))`

DECLARE `The conciliator's recommendation` IS ONE OF
    `the Director should investigate`
    `no recommendation`
    `the Director should not investigate`
    `the parties reached agreement`

DECLARE `After conciliation` IS ONE OF
    `the Director may investigate it, refer it again for conciliation, or deal with it in another way the Act allows (s 43(2))`
    `the Director must not take it further unless you ask; you have asked, so the Director may investigate, refer it again for conciliation, or deal with it in another way (s 43(1))`
    `the Director must not investigate it, refer it again for conciliation or deal with it in any other way, unless you ask (s 43(1), the text's no)`
    `the conciliator's final report records the agreement (s 40(2)(a)); the Act sets no further step for the Director (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Conciliation of my complaint has ended: can it go further (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) ss 40, 43)? If the conciliator recommends no investigation, the Director must not take the complaint further unless you ask; otherwise the Director may investigate, refer it again or deal with it in another way.
GIVEN `the recommendation` IS A `The conciliator's recommendation` @desc What the conciliator's final report says
      `I asked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked the Director to take the complaint further
GIVETH AN `After conciliation`
`s 43 -- after conciliation` `the recommendation` `I asked` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the recommendation` EQUALS `the parties reached agreement`
        THEN `the conciliator's final report records the agreement (s 40(2)(a)); the Act sets no further step for the Director (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF `the recommendation` EQUALS `the Director should not investigate`
         THEN IF `I asked`
              THEN `the Director must not take it further unless you ask; you have asked, so the Director may investigate, refer it again for conciliation, or deal with it in another way (s 43(1))`
              ELSE `the Director must not investigate it, refer it again for conciliation or deal with it in any other way, unless you ask (s 43(1), the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `the Director may investigate it, refer it again for conciliation, or deal with it in another way the Act allows (s 43(2))`

§§ `s 52 -- If I go to court (situation: the user or provider has begun legal proceedings, or a tribunal is involved)`

DECLARE `What happens to my complaint if proceedings are begun` IS ONE OF
    `s 52 does not apply to a complaint about a code of conduct or a prohibition order (s 52(1A), the text's no)`
    `the Director may reopen the complaint with your consent, the proceedings having been withdrawn or abandoned (s 52(3))`
    `the Director must stop dealing with the issue and tell you in writing within 14 days, but may refer the complaint for conciliation, with both your and the provider's consent, until the hearing begins (s 52(1), (4))`
    `the Director must stop dealing with the issue and tell you in writing within 14 days (s 52(1))`
    `the Director may still investigate the matter so as to take part in the further proceedings (s 52(2))`
    `s 52 does not stop the Director: none of its conditions is met (the text's no)`

@export If I (or the provider) start court proceedings, or a tribunal or registration board is involved, what happens to my complaint (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) s 52)? The Director must stop dealing with an issue once legal proceedings on it begin, a registration board has decided it, proceedings are begun in the State Administrative Tribunal or an industrial tribunal, or the Director thinks a court or that Tribunal should deal with it; conciliation can continue with both parties' consent until the hearing begins.
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `What my complaint is about` @desc What your complaint says happened (s 25(1))
      `legal proceedings begun` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (the user) or the provider has begun legal proceedings relating to the issue
      `withdrawn or abandoned` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether those proceedings have since been withdrawn or abandoned
      `both consent to conciliation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If legal proceedings have begun: whether both you and the provider consent to conciliation going on
      `hearing begun` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If legal proceedings have begun: whether the actual hearing of the case has begun
      `board decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a registration board has decided the issue
      `tribunal proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether proceedings on that specific issue have been begun in the State Administrative Tribunal or an industrial tribunal
      `Director thinks a court should deal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Director considers the issue should properly be dealt with by a court or the State Administrative Tribunal
      `Director investigating for those proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Director considers it should investigate the matter to take part in further proceedings about it
GIVETH A `What happens to my complaint if proceedings are begun`
`s 52 -- what happens to my complaint if proceedings are begun` `the matter` `legal proceedings begun` `withdrawn or abandoned` `both consent to conciliation` `hearing begun` `board decided` `tribunal proceedings` `Director thinks a court should deal` `Director investigating for those proceedings` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `a matter in s 25(1)(i) to (k)` `the matter`
        THEN `s 52 does not apply to a complaint about a code of conduct or a prohibition order (s 52(1A), the text's no)`
        IF `legal proceedings begun`
         THEN IF `withdrawn or abandoned`
              THEN `the Director may reopen the complaint with your consent, the proceedings having been withdrawn or abandoned (s 52(3))`
              ELSE IF `both consent to conciliation` AND (NOT `hearing begun`)
                   THEN `the Director must stop dealing with the issue and tell you in writing within 14 days, but may refer the complaint for conciliation, with both your and the provider's consent, until the hearing begins (s 52(1), (4))`
                   ELSE `the Director must stop dealing with the issue and tell you in writing within 14 days (s 52(1))`
        IF `board decided` OR `tribunal proceedings`
         THEN `the Director must stop dealing with the issue and tell you in writing within 14 days (s 52(1))`
        IF `Director thinks a court should deal`
         THEN IF `Director investigating for those proceedings`
              THEN `the Director may still investigate the matter so as to take part in the further proceedings (s 52(2))`
              ELSE `the Director must stop dealing with the issue and tell you in writing within 14 days (s 52(1))`
        OTHERWISE `s 52 does not stop the Director: none of its conditions is met (the text's no)`

§§ `ss 4(4), 36BA, 36C and 42 -- My complaint and a claim in court`

DECLARE `Where it was said` IS ONE OF
    `in the provider's written response to my complaint (s 36A)`
    `in negotiations to settle my complaint (s 36B)`
    `in the conciliation of my complaint`
    `in an investigation by the Director`
    `somewhere else`

@export Can what was said in the complaint process be used as evidence in court or a tribunal, for example in a negligence claim (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) ss 36BA, 36C, 42)? Evidence of anything said in the provider's response, in settlement negotiations or in conciliation is not admissible in proceedings before a court or tribunal. Answers yes or no, or nothing where this Act does not say.
GIVEN `where it was said` IS A `Where it was said` @desc Where the statement or admission was made
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`ss 36BA, 36C, 42 -- can it be used as evidence in court` `where it was said` MEANS
    IF    `where it was said` EQUALS `in the provider's written response to my complaint (s 36A)`
       OR `where it was said` EQUALS `in negotiations to settle my complaint (s 36B)`
       OR `where it was said` EQUALS `in the conciliation of my complaint`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `The duty I would sue on` IS ONE OF
    `the guiding principles for providing health services (s 4(1))`
    `a duty under another law, such as the common law of negligence`

@export Can I sue a provider for breach of statutory duty for not following the guiding principles in the Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) (s 4(4))? No: s 4 creates no duty that can found an action for breach of statutory duty. The Act does not say anything about claims on other duties, such as negligence.
GIVEN `the duty` IS A `The duty I would sue on` @desc The duty you would sue the provider for breaching
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(4) -- can I sue for breach of this duty` `the duty` MEANS
    IF `the duty` EQUALS `the guiding principles for providing health services (s 4(1))`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 71 -- Keeping what I learn confidential`

DECLARE `My part in the complaint process` IS ONE OF
    `a participant in conciliation or an investigation, myself or as someone's agent`
    `a person who received a notice from the Director or the conciliator about a complaint (ss 33, 35, 37, 50)`
    `none of these parts`

DECLARE `Why I would record, disclose or use the information` IS ONE OF
    `in the course of duty`
    `because this Act or another law requires or allows it`
    `for proceedings for an offence against this Act`
    `with the written authority of the Minister or of the person the information is about`
    `in circumstances prescribed by regulations`
    `none of these reasons`

@export May I record, disclose or use information I obtained through the complaint process, including giving it in evidence in court (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA) s 71)? A participant in conciliation or an investigation, or a person given notices about a complaint, must not, except in the course of duty, as a law requires or allows, for prosecutions under the Act, with written authority, or in prescribed circumstances; in court only the last three count. Penalty up to $2 500. Answers yes, no, or nothing where s 71 does not reach you.
GIVEN `my part` IS A `My part in the complaint process` @desc How you came to have the information
      `why` IS A `Why I would record, disclose or use the information` @desc Why you would record, disclose or use it
      `in court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is to be given as evidence, or a document produced, to a court
      `statistical only` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is statistical or other information that could not reasonably be expected to identify anyone
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 71 -- may I record, disclose or use this information` `my part` `why` `in court` `statistical only` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `my part` EQUALS `none of these parts`
        THEN NOTHING
        IF `statistical only`
         THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `why` EQUALS `none of these reasons`
         THEN JUST FALSE
        IF `in court` AND (`why` EQUALS `in the course of duty` OR `why` EQUALS `because this Act or another law requires or allows it`)
         THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `Offences a complainant meets or is protected by`

DECLARE `The conduct` IS ONE OF
    `demanding or receiving payment for acting as a user's representative (s 21)`
    `a provider not giving a written response the Director required (s 36A(2))`
    `a provider not reporting on remedial action in time (s 51(1))`
    `failing to comply with an interim prohibition order (s 52G)`
    `failing to comply with a prohibition order (s 52N)`
    `in this State, breaching an interstate prohibition order or interim prohibition order (s 52Q(2))`
    `making a statement or giving information or an answer for an investigation, known to be false or misleading in a material respect (s 61)`
    `refusing or failing to attend, produce, answer, or be sworn as required by the Director under s 60 (s 62)`
    `recording, disclosing or using information obtained in the complaint process (s 71)`
    `making a statement in a complaint, report or return, known to be false or misleading in a material respect (s 72)`
    `threatening or intimidating someone not to complain, or dismissing them or causing them detriment because they complained or took part (s 73)`

@export What is the most a person can be fined for this conduct connected with a complaint (Health and Disability Services (Complaints) Act 1995 (WA))? Gives the maximum fine in dollars, or 0 where the Act makes the conduct no offence on these facts.
GIVEN `the conduct` IS A `The conduct` @desc The conduct
      `a later offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For s 21 only: whether the person has been convicted of an offence against s 21 before
      `a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For s 52Q only: whether the person is a body corporate (a company) rather than an individual
      `an excuse or exception` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For s 62: whether the person had a lawful excuse; for s 71: whether one of the s 71 exceptions applies (see the s 71 question). Not used for other conduct
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most a person can be fined for this conduct` `the conduct` `a later offence` `a body corporate` `an excuse or exception` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the conduct` EQUALS `demanding or receiving payment for acting as a user's representative (s 21)`
        THEN IF `a later offence` THEN 10000 ELSE 1000
        IF `the conduct` EQUALS `a provider not giving a written response the Director required (s 36A(2))`
         THEN 0
        IF    `the conduct` EQUALS `failing to comply with an interim prohibition order (s 52G)`
            OR `the conduct` EQUALS `failing to comply with a prohibition order (s 52N)`
         THEN 30000
        IF `the conduct` EQUALS `in this State, breaching an interstate prohibition order or interim prohibition order (s 52Q(2))`
         THEN IF `a body corporate` THEN 60000 ELSE 30000
        IF    `the conduct` EQUALS `refusing or failing to attend, produce, answer, or be sworn as required by the Director under s 60 (s 62)`
            OR `the conduct` EQUALS `recording, disclosing or using information obtained in the complaint process (s 71)`
         THEN IF `an excuse or exception` THEN 0 ELSE 2500
        OTHERWISE 2500
